    "answer": {
      "short": "$n=2^k$ for a non-negative integer $k$.\n\nAny odd factor greater than 1 in $n$ would make $2^n+1$ composite.",
      "worked": "Suppose $n$ is not a power of two. Then $n=pq$, where $p\\geq1$ and $q>1$ is odd. Put $A=2^p\\geq2$. The odd-power sum identity gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2^n+1&=A^q+1\\\\2^n+1&=(A+1)(A^{q-1}-A^{q-2}+\\cdots-A+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThe first factor is at least 3. Pairing consecutive terms in the second factor gives\n$$A^{q-2}(A-1)+A^{q-4}(A-1)+\\cdots+A(A-1)+1>1.$$\nBoth factors are integers greater than 1, contradicting primality. Hence $n=2^k$ for some non-negative integer $k$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
